# lib/std/proc.aiw -- child processes: start a program with its three standard
# descriptors where the caller wants them, and wait for it with a limit.
# A program that uses it uses lib/std/io.aiw first. The file asks the
# runtime for everything, so it is the same on every platform.
#
# Every name of this file starts with "proc"; a program has no var of such
# a name (see lib/std/io.aiw).

# the environment of this process as the kernel passed it: the address of
# the addresses of its strings, "NAME=value" with a NUL byte at the end,
# with a 0 after the last
fn procenv
	args as procp
	load procp as procn
	ret procp+16+procn*8
end

# the value of the variable of the environment whose name is the procn
# bytes at procp: its address and length, or 0 and 0 if there is none
fn procvar procp procn
	call procenv as proce
	label next
	load proce as procs
	ifz procs
		ret 0 0
	end
	proci=0
	while proci<procn
		loadb procs+proci as procc
		loadb procp+proci as procd
		jne procc procd no
		proci+=1
	end
	loadb procs+procn as procc
	jne procc 61 no
	procs+=procn+1
	call strlen procs as procl
	ret procs procl
	label no
	proce+=8
	jmp next
end

# Start a program as a child process: its number, or the error of the
# runtime. procargv is the address of the addresses of its arguments,
# strings with a NUL byte at the end, with a 0 after the last; the first
# is the program, a path if it has a slash and else a name looked for in
# the directories of PATH. The child gets the environment of this
# process, and procin, procout and procerr as its descriptors 0, 1 and 2;
# -1 leaves the one of this process. The caller keeps and closes the
# descriptors it passed, and ends the child with procwait. A program that
# cannot be started is a child that ends with status 127, and so is one
# whose descriptors cannot be put in place. The three may be given in any
# order: 2 as procout and 1 as procerr swaps them.
fn procstart procargv procin procout procerr
	local procm 24
	store procm procin
	store procm+8 procout
	store procm+16 procerr
	rt start procargv 0 procm 0 as procpid
	ret procpid
end

# Start a program as procstart does, held to this process: it is killed
# when the thread that started it ends, however it ends, so a program
# that is itself killed leaves no child of this kind behind. What the
# child starts is not held by this; a child that holds its own children
# the same way takes them with it. A program that changes its user or
# group when it starts (set-user-ID) is not held. Where the platform
# refuses to hold the child (on Linux a filter of system calls that
# denies prctl), the program is not started and the child ends with
# status 127, as one whose program cannot be started.
fn procheld procargv procin procout procerr
	local procm 24
	store procm procin
	store procm+8 procout
	store procm+16 procerr
	rt start procargv 0 procm 1 as procpid
	ret procpid
end

# Wait for the child procpid to end and take it: its exit status, 0 to
# 255; or the signal that ended it, negated; or -1000 if it still ran
# after procms milliseconds, in which case it was killed and taken; or
# -1001 if there is no such child. procms 0 waits without limit.
fn procwait procpid procms
	rt reap procpid procms as procr
	ret procr
end

# the time of the clock that only goes forward (CLOCK_MONOTONIC), in
# nanoseconds since a moment that is the same for the whole run
fn procnow
	rt clock as procn
	ret procn
end

# Let this thread, and the children it starts from now on, run only on
# the processor proccpu, or on the lowest one it may run on if that is
# not among them: the processor it is now bound to, or the error of the
# runtime. Processors are numbered from 0, below 1024.
fn procpin proccpu
	rt pin proccpu as procr
	ret procr
end
